1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,604
Proofsheet is an analyst that shows its work.

2
00:00:03,554 --> 00:00:11,587
You drop a messy export, approve the business definition, and get a
report where every number and every name carries a receipt.

3
00:00:12,189 --> 00:00:15,667
A normal AI data tool can make a chart.

4
00:00:15,618 --> 00:00:23,573
It cannot tell you which definition it used, which rows it dropped, or
whether the number is the one your finance team would defend.

5
00:00:23,573 --> 00:00:29,367
Cleaning still takes most of the time, and one wrong figure is enough
to lose the room.

6
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:32,582
The product has four moves.

7
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:35,149
Profile the file in Rust.

8
00:00:35,149 --> 00:00:37,923
Lock the definition with the user.

9
00:00:37,923 --> 00:00:41,243
Execute in a disposable sandbox.

10
00:00:41,243 --> 00:00:43,210
Prove the result.

11
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:47,766
The model can plan and write code, but it never authors a number.

12
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:52,141
The definition lock fixes the right-answer problem.

13
00:00:52,091 --> 00:00:54,084
Shipping is out.

14
00:00:54,084 --> 00:00:57,195
Cancelled and refunded orders are out.

15
00:00:57,195 --> 00:00:59,903
Missing values are excluded.

16
00:00:59,903 --> 00:01:02,820
Discounts are read as fractions.

17
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:07,287
Ambiguous dates follow one explicit format precedence.

18
00:01:07,287 --> 00:01:10,685
The model cannot change these after approval.

19
00:01:11,299 --> 00:01:14,088
Containment is part of the product.

20
00:01:14,037 --> 00:01:21,408
Every task gets a fresh sandbox with no network, no secrets, a
read-only root, and hard caps.

21
00:01:21,408 --> 00:01:30,197
The drill runs a real destructive command, a fork storm, a runaway
loop, a credential scan, a network probe, and a host probe.

22
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:33,972
The host canary is intact after all six.

23
00:01:34,569 --> 00:01:38,073
The report note can only cite placeholders.

24
00:01:38,024 --> 00:01:44,390
Rust resolves them from the execution ledger and audits the chart
series against the same numbers.

25
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:46,864
Six claims are receipted.

26
00:01:46,864 --> 00:01:49,299
Zero unverified.

27
00:01:49,299 --> 00:01:52,372
The chart audit passes eight checks.

28
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,072
A second model, which never sees the analyst script, recomputes the
metrics in a separate sandbox.

29
00:02:00,022 --> 00:02:06,051
Both import the same parsing contract, so the check is about
aggregation and logic.

30
00:02:06,051 --> 00:02:12,756
It caught two real ambiguities in our own analysis: discount semantics
and date precedence.

31
00:02:12,756 --> 00:02:15,516
Both became locked assumptions.

32
00:02:16,129 --> 00:02:18,891
You can also try to make it lie.

33
00:02:18,842 --> 00:02:25,117
Inject a fake forty-seven percent and an unknown metric, and the
verifier flags both.

34
00:02:25,117 --> 00:02:27,358
The stamp is withheld.

35
00:02:27,358 --> 00:02:30,312
The production report is untouched.

36
00:02:30,312 --> 00:02:33,751
The challenge is a live check on the same ledger.

37
00:02:34,359 --> 00:02:37,030
The second month is one click.

38
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:43,347
The saved definition, script and template replay on a new export with
no model in the loop.

39
00:02:43,347 --> 00:02:51,537
The comparison is computed in Rust: same rows, net revenue up three
percent, peak month and top country updated.

40
00:02:52,139 --> 00:03:03,872
Proofsheet is real today: definition lock, sandboxed execution, chart
audit, independent execution, containment drill, assurance bundle, and

41
00:03:03,822 --> 00:03:09,070
Next is authentication, scheduling, and one microVM per task.

42
00:03:09,070 --> 00:03:12,742
The live app and repository are in the submission.

